## `>_` WHAT IS WEBLOOM

Webloom is an AI-powered website engineering pipeline that turns raw business
information into structured, validated website intelligence.

Instead of firing one giant prompt at an LLM and praying to the JSON gods,
Webloom splits the workflow into discrete services — each with an explicit
contract, a defined input/output shape, and a validation boundary.

```
  BUSINESS
     │
     ▼
  DATA EXTRACTION
     │
     ▼
  BUSINESS INTELLIGENCE
     │
     ▼
  BRAND DNA
     │
     ▼
  WEBSITE STRATEGY
     │
     ▼
  LANDING PAGE SPECIFICATION
     │
     ▼
  VALIDATION
     │
     ▼
  WEBSITE GENERATION
```

**Engineering focus:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| 🧩 Service architecture | Modular, single-responsibility services |
| 🤖 AI orchestration | Structured, schema-checked AI output |
| 🚧 Validation boundaries | Every stage validated before the next runs |
| 🔒 State isolation | No cross-contamination between business runs |
| 💥 Failure handling | Fail fast, fail loud, fail debuggably |
| 🧪 Pipeline testing | Coverage for failure modes, not just happy paths |
| 🔍 Debuggability | Every intermediate structure is inspectable |

<br>

## `>_` REQUIREMENTS

| Dependency | Version | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Node.js** | `18+` | Recommended |
| **npm** | — | Ships with Node.js |
| **Git** | — | Required |
| **OmniRoute API Key** | — | **You must bring your own.** Webloom does not ship with one. |

<br>

## `>_` QUICK START

### `[ macOS / Linux ]`

```bash
# 01 — Clone the repo
git clone https://github.com/notbeingsuraj/SiteForge.git
cd SiteForge

# 02 — Install dependencies
npm install                 # if the repo uses npm workspaces
# — or, if working directly inside the API —
cd apps/api && npm install

# 03 — Configure environment
cp .env.example .env        # if .env.example doesn't exist: touch .env

# then add your key to .env:
echo "OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=your_omniroute_api_key_here" >> .env

# 04 — Start the API
cd apps/api
npm start                   # production
npm run dev                 # development (hot reload)

# 05 — Test the pipeline
node test_full_pipeline_fixed.js
# — or run a targeted debug script —
node test_landing_page_debug2.js
```

### `[ Windows / PowerShell ]`

```powershell
# 01 — Clone the repo
git clone https://github.com/notbeingsuraj/SiteForge.git
cd SiteForge

# 02 — Install dependencies
npm install                          # if the repo uses npm workspaces
# — or, if working directly inside the API —
cd apps/api; npm install

# 03 — Configure environment
Copy-Item .env.example .env          # if .env.example doesn't exist:
New-Item .env -ItemType File         # ^ use this instead

# then add your key to .env:
Add-Content .env "OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=your_omniroute_api_key_here"

# 04 — Start the API
cd apps/api
npm start                            # production
npm run dev                          # development (hot reload)

# 05 — Test the pipeline
node test_full_pipeline_fixed.js
```

> ⚠️ **Add any additional API/model environment variables your local config
> requires to the same `.env` file.**

<br>

## `>_` OMNIROUTE SETUP

Webloom uses **OmniRoute** as its AI routing layer:

```
   WEBLOOM
       │
       ▼
   OMNIROUTE API
       │
       ├──▶ AI MODEL
       ├──▶ AI MODEL
       └──▶ AI MODEL
```

1. Create/configure your own OmniRoute API credentials.
2. Place the key in your local `.env`:

   ```env
   OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=YOUR_KEY_HERE
   ```

3. **Never commit `.env` to Git.** Your `.gitignore` should contain:

   ```gitignore
   .env
   .env.*
   !.env.example
   ```

> 🚨 Do not replace `YOUR_KEY_HERE` with a real key inside the repository —
> unless you enjoy donating your API budget to strangers.

<br>

## `>_` RUN THE PIPELINE

From `SiteForge/apps/api`, run:

```bash
node test_full_pipeline_fixed.js
```

A healthy pipeline looks like this:

```
