<br>

## `>_` DESIGN PRINCIPLES

| # | Principle |
|---|---|
| 01 | AI generates. **Software validates.** |
| 02 | Invalid data stops early. |
| 03 | Services own specific responsibilities. |
| 04 | Intermediate structures remain inspectable. |
| 05 | Business state must remain isolated. |
| 06 | Failures are expected system states. |
| 07 | Tests cover failure modes, not only happy paths. |
| 08 | A successful AI response ≠ a successful software operation. |

<br>

## `>_` DEVELOPMENT

```bash
# status & history
git status
git log --oneline -5
git pull

# start a feature branch
git checkout -b feature/your-feature

# install & test
npm install
npm test
npm run dev

# ship it
git add .
git commit -m "describe your change"
git push origin feature/your-feature
```

> 💡 Prefer feature branches over committing straight to `main` —
> it's a great way to discover why teams invented branches in the first place.

